steps.body:
A linked list stores each value in its own **node**, and each node points to the next one. The list itself is just a pointer to the first node, called the **head**. An empty list is `head == nullptr`.

```cpp
struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;   // nullptr marks the last node
};

Node* head = nullptr;                // empty list
Node* node = new Node{7, nullptr};   // one heap node
```

Every `new` is a separate heap allocation, so nodes end up scattered across the heap, with no promise that they sit side by side like a vector's elements. Walking a list jumps from address to address, which is slower than reading a vector front to back. To append in O(1), keep a `tail` pointer too: the first node sets both `head` and `tail`, and later nodes hang off `tail->next`.

```cpp
if (head == nullptr) {
    head = node;
} else {
    tail->next = node;
}
tail = node;
```

Walk the list with `for (Node* cur = head; cur != nullptr; cur = cur->next)`. When you are done, `delete` every node. Save `next` before the `delete`, because reading `cur->next` after `delete cur` is use after free.

Real code owns nodes with `std::unique_ptr` or a class whose destructor frees them. Raw `Node*` shows the mechanics and is still right for traversal that does not own anything. The standard `std::list` is a ready-made doubly linked list, but a `std::vector` is usually faster because its elements are contiguous.

example.code:
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

struct Node {
    int value;
    Node* next;
};

void printList(const Node* head) {
    for (const Node* cur = head; cur != nullptr; cur = cur->next) {
        std::cout << cur->value << " -> ";
    }
    std::cout << "null\n";
}

void freeList(Node* head) {
    while (head != nullptr) {
        Node* next = head->next;
        delete head;
        head = next;
    }
}

int main() {
    const std::vector<int> values = {1, 2, 3};
    Node* head = nullptr;
    Node* tail = nullptr;
    for (int v : values) {
        Node* node = new Node{v, nullptr};
        if (head == nullptr) {
            head = node;
        } else {
            tail->next = node;
        }
        tail = node;
    }
    printList(head);

    int length = 0;
    int sum = 0;
    for (Node* cur = head; cur != nullptr; cur = cur->next) {
        ++length;
        sum += cur->value;
    }
    std::cout << "length " << length << '\n';
    std::cout << "sum " << sum << '\n';

    freeList(head);
    return 0;
}

