steps.body:
Reversing a list creates no new nodes. Every node stays at the same heap address, and you only flip each `next` pointer to aim backward. Three pointers do it in one pass: `prev` (the reversed part), `cur` (the node being flipped), and `next` (the rest of the list).

```cpp
Node* reverseList(Node* head) {
    Node* prev = nullptr;
    Node* cur = head;
    while (cur != nullptr) {
        Node* next = cur->next;
        cur->next = prev;
        prev = cur;
        cur = next;
    }
    return prev;
}
```

Order matters inside the loop. Save `next` first, because `cur->next = prev` overwrites the only link to the rest of the list. When `cur` falls off the end, `prev` holds the old tail, which is the new head, so return it.

The caller must write `head = reverseList(head);`. The old head is now the last node, so forgetting the assignment prints a one-node list and loses the rest. Draw three boxes and their arrows on paper before coding pointer rewiring like this. It is O(n) time and O(1) extra memory.

